<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsaparang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsaparang is the ruined capital of the kingdom of Guge, built on a ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> valley in Zanda County, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Its palaces, temples and cave dwellings rise up an eroded hillside, and its temples hold fine murals of the 15th and 16th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-andrade">António de Andrade</a> founded a mission there in 1624, and the city declined after Ladakh conquered Guge around 1630.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsaparang.jpg" alt="Tsaparang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: yuen yan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngari_Tsaparang_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tsaparang lies in the Garuda Valley of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej River</a> in Ngari Prefecture, near the border of Ladakh, 26 km west of the 11th-century monastery of Tholing and not far west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kailash">Mount Kailash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-manasarovar">Lake Manasarovar</a>. The fortress is perched on a pyramid-shaped rock about 152 to 183 metres high at the end of a long, narrow spur, and is riddled with tunnels and caves cut into the rock. Nearby is the Bon monastery of Gurugem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to some accounts Tsaparang was made the capital of Guge by Namde Wosung, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a>, the anti-Buddhist king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, after Langdarma's assassination; others say Langdarma's grandsons fled to western Tibet around 919. Guge controlled an ancient trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Tibet and was a regional power by the 10th century. In the 11th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeshe-o">Yeshe-Ö</a>, with the translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinchen-zangpo">Rinchen Zangpo</a> and the Indian master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atiśa</a>, reintroduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to western Tibet.</p><p>In the summer of 1624 the Portuguese Jesuits António de Andrade and Manuel Marques, the first known Europeans to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, reached Tsaparang and gained the king's leave to preach. With three more Jesuits they built a church at the foot of the citadel and another at Rudok. In 1630 Senge Namgyal of Ladakh overthrew Guge and took its pro-Jesuit king, Tri Tashi Drakpa, captive, and the mission fell into disarray. In 1679–80 Tsaparang and Guge were conquered by Central Tibet under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The common people lived in a village at the base of the rock; above it stand two public temples, the Lhakhang Marpo (Red Chapel) and Lhakhang Karpo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-chapel">White Chapel</a>), with the monks' quarters, and higher still, reached by a twisting stone staircase in a tunnel, the royal quarters and at the very top the summer palace. Despite the damage of 1679–80, and the destruction of most of the statues and murals of both chapels by the Red Guards during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, many frescoes survive. The historian Michael Wood has suggested that Tsaparang was the historical origin of the legend of Shangri-La.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsaparang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His territories were divided among his sons, and one line ruled Guge and Purang in the upper Sutlej valley, later with its capital at <b>Tsaparang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/guge">Guge</a></li></ul></section>
